'''HMS ''Apollo''''', the sixth ship of the [[Royal Navy]] to be named for the Greek god [[Apollo]], was a second-class {{sclass|Apollo|cruiser|0}} [[protected cruiser]] launched in 1891 and converted to a [[minelayer]] in 1909 along with six of her sisters. They formed a minelaying squadron in 1914–15 during the [[First World War]], although ''Apollo'' was disarmed in 1915 and served in secondary roles until sold for breaking up in 1920.

==Armament==
{{stack|[[File:Apollo class cruiser diagrams Brasseys 1897.jpg|thumb|Diagram of an ''Apollo''-class cruiser]]}}
''Apollo'' carried two [[QF 6 inch Mk I - III naval gun|QF {{convert|6|in|mm|adj=on|sigfig=4}} guns]], one on the forecastle and the other on the [[Stern|poop]] and six [[QF 4.7 inch Gun Mk I - IV|QF 4.7-inch (120 mm) guns]] mounted in the waist. Four single [[QF 6-pounder Hotchkiss]] guns were carried in the waist, two more on the forecastle and two on the poop. Two {{convert|0.45|in|abbr=on}} [[Nordenfelt gun|Nordenfelt]] 5 barrel [[machine gun]]s were mounted in the forecastle and two in the poop. One [[QF 3-pounder Hotchkiss]] gun was carried for use in the ship's boats, although it was provided one mount on each side of the after end of the waist. Four {{convert|14|in|abbr=on}} [[torpedo tube]] were fitted, two fixed on the stem and stern, and two broadside training tubes on the upper deck aft.<ref name=r6>Roberts, p. 36</ref>

She was totally disarmed when she was converted to a [[minelayer]] in 1909 at [[Chatham Dockyard]]. Mine rails were fitted on her maindeck, which required that the cabins under the poop be removed, and she could carry a hundred [[naval mine|mines]]. Her after deckhouse was extended and her magazines were converted to storerooms.<ref name="r6" />

When the First World War began she was given four QF {{convert|4.7|in|mm|adj=on}} guns, but these were again removed when she was disarmed in 1915.<ref name="r6" />

==Protection==
''Apollo''{{'}}s armored [[Deck (ship)|deck]]s ranged from {{convert|2|-|1.25|in|mm}} in thickness and her [[conning tower]] was {{convert|3|in|mm}} thick. Her engine hatch was {{convert|5|in}} thick.<ref name="r6"/>

==Propulsion==
Apollo had 2-shaft steam [[reciprocating engine]]s and five boilers that gave 7000 indicated horsepower (ihp) using natural draught and {{convert|9000|ihp|abbr=on}} using forced draught. She could make {{convert|18.5|kn|mph km/h|lk=in}} using natural draught and{{convert|20|kn|mph km/h|lk=in}} at forced draught. She carried {{convert|535|LT|t}} of coal.<ref name="r6"/>

==History==
Originally under Captain Richard Poore, she came under the captaincy of Captain (later Vice Admiral) [[George Anson Primrose]] in 1893.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.dreadnoughtproject.org/tfs/index.php/George_Anson_Primrose|title=George Anson Primrose - the Dreadnought Project}}</ref>

After a spell in the reserve, she was present at the [[Fleet Review]] for the [[Diamond Jubilee]] in 1897, and was transferred back to the reserve in 1900. In February that year she was moved from [[HMNB Chatham|Chatham]] to [[HMNB Devonport|Devonport]].<ref>{{Cite newspaper The Times |title=Naval & Military intelligence|date=20 February 1900 |page=9 |issue=36070}}</ref> She took part in the [[Fleet review (Commonwealth realms)|fleet review]] held at [[Spithead]] on 16 August 1902 for the [[Coronation of King Edward VII and Queen Alexandra|coronation]] of King [[Edward VII]].<ref>{{Cite newspaper The Times |title=The Coronation – Naval Review |date=13 August 1902 |page=4 |issue=36845}}</ref> In 1909 she was converted into a [[minelayer]], and served in this role for the first year of the First World War. She and her sisters formed a squadron operating from [[Dover]] and the [[Nore]]. She was again disarmed in 1915 and either relegated to secondary roles or laid up, with [[Arthur Wood (Royal Navy officer)|Arthur Wood]] captaining her between 1917 and 1919. 

In August 1920, ''Apollo'' was sold, along with her sister [[HMS Andromache (1890)|''Andromache'']], to Sidney Castle and moved to Corporation Wharf in the [[Cattewater]] for demolition.<ref name="r6"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Shipbreaking Scheme |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000329/19200824/090/0005 |access-date=8 January 2025 |work=Western Morning News |issue=18868 |date=24 August 1920 |location=Plymouth |page=5|via=British Newspaper Archive}}</ref> The two cruisers were stripped to their hulls, but then registered as merchant vessels by Castle and offered for sale.<ref>{{cite book |title=Mercantile Navy List |date=1923 |publisher=Board of Trade |location=London |page=23 |url=https://www.crewlist.org.uk/data/viewimages?name=apollo&year=1923&steamsail=Steam&year=1923 |access-date=8 January 2025|via=CLIP}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Lyme Regis Council |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000265/19220411/062/0005 |access-date=8 January 2025 |work=Western Times |issue=ss694 |date=11 April 1922 |location=Exeter |page=5|via=British Newspaper Archive}}</ref> Unsold, ''Apollo'' was broken up by Autumn 1925.


